<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William II of Sicily</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1153–1189</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as William the Good</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William II, called the Good, was the Norman King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from 1166 to 1189. His fleets campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zakynthos</a> and sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> in 1185, and supported Isaac Komnenos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. He built the cathedral of Monreale, and his childless death opened the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-sicily.jpg" alt="William II of Sicily" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Luiz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dedication_mosaic_-_Cathedral_of_Monreale_-_Italy_2015_(crop).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
